<%# "THIS PERIOD" — SPENDING AS PROGRESS, ON THE CLAIM (answers-first spec §4, computed-claims
    §3.4). Local: `presenter` (HomePresenter).

    ** EVERY FIGURE HERE IS A CLAIM NOW, AND NOT A HOLDING. ** The rows used to read a
    `HoldingCalculator` — what had been ALLOCATED into a category, less what was spent out of it — and
    a per-period plan derived from `Budget#steady_ask`. Nothing moves any more (spec §5), so there is
    nothing allocated to read: each line comes off ONE `ClaimCalculator`, from the page's ONE
    `ClaimLedger`, and says the one sentence §3.4 gives its shape.

      RATE      $310.00 of $400.00                     spent of this period's rate, over in red
      ACCRUING  $450.00 built up of $1,200.00          the running total against the target,
                next due Mar 1 · $200.00 per period      with the schedule underneath
      UNRULED   spent $32.00                           the fact, no bar, no pressure

    ** A ROW PER CATEGORY, A LINE PER RULE (the ruling — see HomePresenter::PeriodRow). ** §3.4's
    sentences are per RULE and `Category#claim` is a SUM, so a category carrying a rate rule beside an
    item-backed bill cannot honestly print one figure: the two are denominated in different things.
    Where a category has exactly one rule — the ordinary shape — the row renders as it always did.

    THE WORDS ARE THE USER'S: spent, of, built up. "Set aside" is gone with the movements it named. %>
<section class="bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded" data-this-period>
  <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-200">
    <%# THE HERO'S OWN PERIOD READER, so the days-left figure here and the one under the hero's bar
        cannot come from two different windows. Silent for a user who has declared no period — the
        same refusal `#period_progress` makes about the same reader — because "18 days left" would
        state a boundary nobody set. %>
    <h3 class="text-sm font-semibold text-gray-900" data-this-period-heading>
      This period<% if progress = presenter.period_progress %> · <%= pluralize(progress.days_left, "day") %> left<% end %>
    </h3>
  </div>

  <% presenter.period_rows.each do |row| %>
    <%# `data-period-row` IS THE HOOK EVERY COPY ASSERTION GOES THROUGH, named by the category so a
        two-row screen cannot be satisfied by one row's figures. %>
    <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0" data-period-row="<%= row.category.name %>">
      <% if row.budgeted? %>
        <% row.lines.each_with_index do |line, index| %>
          <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4 <%= "mt-2" unless index.zero? %>">
            <%# THE NAME IS THE CATEGORY'S ON THE FIRST LINE AND THE RULE'S ON EVERY OTHER. One rule
                is the ordinary shape and its row reads as a category; a second line is an exception
                carved out of the same category (§3.1's lane partition), so it names the rule that
                carved it — `pool_rule_label`, the app's one answer to what a rule is called. %>
            <span class="<%= index.zero? ? "font-medium text-gray-900" : "text-sm text-gray-600 pl-4" %>">
              <%= index.zero? ? row.category.name : pool_rule_label(line.rule) %>
            </span>

            <%# THE FIGURE (§3.4). Red when the spending has passed what the rule had — `over?` off
                the calculator, which reads the figure BEFORE the clamp at zero and is the only
                reader that can tell "spent it exactly" from "spent more than there was". %>
            <span class="text-sm tabular-nums <%= line.over? ? "text-status-danger font-medium" : "text-gray-700" %>"
                  data-period-figure="<%= pool_rule_label(line.rule) %>">
              <%= claim_figure(line) %>
            </span>
          </div>

          <%# THE TRACK AND THE FILL ARE `_hero`'S AND `categories/_partials/_category_card`'S, so the
              app's bars are one shape. `role="progressbar"` because a bar with no accessible value is
              decoration to a screen reader, and the figure beside it is the value in words either
              way. `aria-label` names which category, since a page of bars is otherwise a page of
              "50%".

              THE PERCENT RIDES ON THE TRACK AND THE COLOUR ON THE FILL, deliberately: a 0% fill has
              no width, so Capybara does not consider it visible, and a hook on it would be
              unassertable on exactly the row a bar most needs pinning — the one nobody has spent
              from. %>
          <% if line.bar? %>
            <div class="mt-1.5 h-1.5 bg-gray-200 rounded-full overflow-hidden"
                 role="progressbar"
                 aria-valuenow="<%= line.percent %>"
                 aria-valuemin="0"
                 aria-valuemax="100"
                 aria-label="<%= row.category.name %>"
                 data-period-bar="<%= line.percent %>">
              <div class="h-1.5 rounded-full <%= line.over? ? "bg-status-danger" : "bg-brand-dark" %>"
                   style="width: <%= line.percent %>%;"
                   data-period-fill></div>
            </div>
          <% end %>

          <%# THE CLAUSE UNDER THE BAR, WHERE IT EARNS ITS PLACE. Two kinds, and never both: what is
              WRONG (over by $X, or an overdue date) takes the line in red, because that is the news
              and it is the same string the trouble strip prints about this rule inches above; the
              SCHEDULE otherwise (`next due Mar 1 · $200.00 per period`), which is the half of §3.4's
              accruing row that the figure has no room for. A rate rule in good standing has neither
              and renders no element at all. %>
          <% if line.trouble? %>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-status-danger font-medium" data-period-clause>
              <%= claim_trouble_label(line) %>
            </p>
          <% elsif schedule = claim_schedule(line) %>
            <p class="mt-1 text-sm text-gray-500" data-period-clause><%= schedule %></p>
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      <% else %>
        <%# A HOLDER WITH NO RULE AT ALL. Nothing claims this money, so there is nothing for a bar to
            be a fraction of — the fact, said the way an unbudgeted row says it, because it IS the
            same fact. %>
        <div class="flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4">
          <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= row.category.name %></span>
          <span class="text-sm text-gray-700 tabular-nums" data-period-figure>spent <%= number_to_currency(row.spent) %></span>
        </div>
      <% end %>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# UNBUDGETED CATEGORIES WITH SPENDING (answers-first §4): the fact, no bar, no pressure. No rule
      claims their receipts, so there is nothing for a bar to be a fraction of and a denominator here
      would be inventing the pressure. Zero-spend ones are absent by construction — the Categories
      page remains the full index. %>
  <% presenter.unbudgeted_rows.each do |row| %>
    <div class="px-6 py-3 border-b border-gray-100 last:border-b-0 flex justify-between items-baseline gap-4"
         data-unbudgeted-row="<%= row.category.name %>">
      <span class="font-medium text-gray-900"><%= row.category.name %></span>
      <span class="text-sm text-gray-700 tabular-nums">spent <%= number_to_currency(row.spent) %></span>
    </div>
  <% end %>

  <%# Said plainly rather than left as an empty card: a blank panel on a money screen reads as
      something that failed to load. The door is the Budget page, which is where a rule — the thing
      that gives a category a claim — is written. %>
  <% if presenter.period_rows.empty? && presenter.unbudgeted_rows.empty? %>
    <p class="px-6 py-3 text-sm text-gray-500">
      Nothing is budgeted yet, and nothing has been spent this period.
      <%= link_to "Give a category a rule on the Budget page", budget_page_path, class: "text-status-info hover:underline" %>
    </p>
  <% end %>
</section>
